# Never rebuild aws-lc (locked FIPS module)

`aws-lc-fips-sys` is **not** an ordinary Rust dependency. It compiles a large
C / CMake / Go-assembly cryptographic module — the **CMVP-validated AWS-LC FIPS
module (cert #4759)** that [`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc)
§4 pins as the Track-A boundary. The workspace consumes it as a **locked,
prebuilt version from Amazon**: the module version never changes, because the
whole point of pinning a validated module is that its bytes are fixed. Nothing
we do to our own Rust source can change what that module contains, so
**rebuilding produces a bit-identical result and buys nothing** — it only costs
multiple minutes of wall clock, every time, on every developer and lab host.

## The rule

> **Never cause `aws-lc-fips-sys` to rebuild. It is a locked prebuilt FIPS
> module; its output cannot change. If a build of it is ever genuinely
> unavoidable, build it exactly **once** per `(profile, target)` and then never
> again — preserve the resulting
> `target/<profile>/build/aws-lc-fips-sys-*/out/` tree forever.**

## Why it happens

Cargo keys every build-script execution directory on a metadata hash
(`target/<profile>/build/aws-lc-fips-sys-<HASH>/out/...`). If `<HASH>` changes,
Cargo re-runs the build script from scratch and leaves the old directory as dead
disk. The hash comes from the *build environment*, not the pinned crate source:

| Invalidator | Why it flips the hash |
| --- | --- |
| `.cargo/config.toml` `rustflags` / `linker` changes (e.g. toggling `rust-lld`) | Part of every unit's metadata hash — full-graph invalidation |
| `RUSTFLAGS` / `CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS` set for some invocations and not others | Same, but flips back and forth — rebuilds on every alternation |
| `[profile.dev]` / `[profile.release]` edits (`debug`, `opt-level`, `codegen-units`, `lto`) | Profile settings feed the hash |
| Dependency / `[patch]` churn changing feature unification reaching `aws-lc-fips-sys` | Enabled feature set is part of the hash |
| An uncommitted per-developer `.cargo/config.local.toml` appearing/disappearing | Silently flips rustflags between runs |
| `cargo clean`, deleting `target/` | Destroys the artifacts outright |
| Changing `CC` / `CXX` / `CMAKE` / `GOPATH` / `AWS_LC_*` | The build script declares `rerun-if-env-changed` on these |

A healthy host accumulates **at most one** `aws-lc-fips-sys-<hash>/out` directory
per `(profile, target)`. Many of them means the workflow has been thrashing, and
each extra directory is several wasted minutes.

## Diagnostic

```powershell
Get-ChildItem target\debug\build -Directory -Filter "aws-lc-fips-sys-*" |
  Where-Object { Test-Path (Join-Path $_.FullName "out\build\artifacts") } |
  Select-Object Name, LastWriteTime
```

Run this before and after any build-configuration change. If the count
increased, the change was an invalidator — say so plainly rather than absorbing
it silently.

## Forbidden actions

- **`cargo clean`** in any repo under `c:\analog-pim\`, for any reason. No
  development problem's correct solution is wiping `target/`. If a cache truly
  looks corrupt, delete the *specific* crate's fingerprint directory
  (`target/<profile>/.fingerprint/<crate>-*`) and never `aws-lc-fips-sys-*`.
- **Deleting `target/**/aws-lc-fips-sys-*`**, its `out/`, or its
  `build/artifacts/` tree — including "disk cleanup" sweeps.
- **Deleting staged FIPS DLLs** (`target/<profile>/aws_lc_fips_*_crypto.dll` and
  the `deps/` copy) — see [`fips-dll-staging.mdc`](fips-dll-staging.mdc).
- **Gratuitous `rustflags` / `linker` / `[profile.*]` churn.** These settings are
  committed precisely so they stay put. A single experiment costs a full-graph
  rebuild in **both** directions (once to apply, once to revert).
- **Setting `RUSTFLAGS` in a shell, script, or task** for a one-off build. Put
  the flag in the committed `.cargo/config.toml` or do not use it — per
  [`no-environment-variables.mdc`](no-environment-variables.mdc), env vars are
  not a configuration surface here anyway.
- **Feature toggling that changes `aws-lc-fips-sys`'s own feature set.** FIPS
  posture is hard-coded, not a Cargo feature, so nothing legitimate to toggle.
  (Crate-local features that do not reach `aws-lc-fips-sys`, such as
  `skip-ui-build`, are fine.)

## Allowed

- **Exactly one** build per `(profile, target)` pair on first use of that
  combination on a host — the "unavoidable, build it once" case.
- Keeping **both** the `dev` and `release` aws-lc trees warm simultaneously.
  Do not delete one to make room for the other.
- A deliberate, announced, one-time invalidation when a build-config change is
  genuinely required. Announce the cost up front, make the change once, commit
  it, and do not revert casually.

## Reverting a past invalidator is usually the wrong fix

If a previous commit already paid for a rebuild and the new fingerprint is now
stable and cached, **leave it alone** — reverting forces a *second* full rebuild
to get back to the old hash. The cost is sunk; stop churning rather than undoing.
Only revert if the setting is actively flipping the hash on repeated runs.

